<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c1" style="--acc:#75435B;--acc-line:#75435B;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#75435B 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c1" data-screen-label="c1-rank-or-distance">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><line x1="4" y1="33" x2="36" y2="33"/><circle cx="13" cy="24" r="9"/><circle cx="31" cy="15" r="4"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C1 · Rank or Distance?</span><h2 class="lens-title">When a figure is larger, is it nearer or more important?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Seeing that one figure is larger is a visible fact. Deciding whether it seems nearer or more emphasized depends on the other relationships around it.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One size relationship · two kinds of support</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> The figure-size ratio stays the same. Notice where the figures meet the ground, where the room's lines lead, and what surrounds the larger figure. How does the larger figure read differently in the two scenes?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="comparison cols-2">
<div><p class="scene-label">Scene A</p><div class="scene landscape" style="background:#d5cfc4 url('/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-neutral-room-v1.png') center/cover no-repeat" role="img" aria-label="Two repeated blue-gray studio maquettes appear in a shallow room. A larger maquette stands low at the left. A smaller maquette stands higher in the field with its feet at the point where floor seams meet.">
<svg class="construction" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 667 L512 520 L1000 667" fill="rgba(228,216,195,.24)"/><path d="M55 667 L512 520 M305 667 L512 520 M720 667 L512 520 M955 667 L512 520" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.5)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M95 630 L912 630 M168 598 L842 598 M255 570 L758 570 M355 544 L660 544" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.42)" stroke-width="3"/></svg>
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:23%;left:8%;bottom:1%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-maquette-figure-cutout-v2.png" alt="" />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:9.6%;left:46.5%;bottom:20%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-maquette-figure-cutout-v2.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M70 667 L512 520 M940 667 L512 520"/><circle class="marker" cx="512" cy="520" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="512" y="520">1</text><path class="guide-solid" d="M86 80 h-18 v545 h18 M500 288 h-18 v232 h18"/><circle class="marker" cx="68" cy="368" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="68" y="368">2</text></svg>
</div></div>
<div><p class="scene-label">Scene B</p><div class="scene landscape" style="background:#d5cfc4 url('/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-neutral-room-v1.png') center/cover no-repeat" role="img" aria-label="The same larger and smaller blue-gray studio maquettes meet one shared ground line in the same shallow room. The larger maquette is centered within a flat vertical color field while the smaller maquette stands to one side.">
<svg class="construction" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="310" y="0" width="380" height="667" fill="rgba(206,129,65,.14)"/><path d="M85 625 H915" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.55)" stroke-width="4"/></svg>
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:23%;left:38.5%;bottom:1%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-maquette-figure-cutout-v2.png" alt="" />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:9.6%;left:76%;bottom:3.7%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-maquette-figure-cutout-v2.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="guide-line" x="310" y="4" width="380" height="657"/><circle class="marker" cx="500" cy="40" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="500" y="40">3</text><path class="guide-line" d="M85 625 H915"/><circle class="marker" cx="820" cy="625" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="820" y="625">4</text></svg>
</div></div>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Recession</strong> — floor seams meet at the smaller maquette's feet.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Relative size</strong> — the larger maquette occupies more of the field.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Central field</strong> — a flat vertical color field singles out the larger maquette.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>4 · Shared ground</strong> — both maquettes meet the same ground line.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In both scenes, the larger maquette occupies more of the pictorial field. In Scene A, <strong>1 the floor seams meet at the smaller maquette's feet</strong>; this recession system and the different ground positions work with <strong>2 relative size</strong> to support a depth inference. In Scene B, <strong>3 a central color field</strong> and <strong>4 a shared ground line</strong> make the larger maquette more visually prominent without supplying the same evidence of distance. Prominence is visible; deciding that it represents social, sacred, or narrative importance would require context.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. In Scene A, locator 1 follows floor seams that meet at the smaller maquette's feet; locator 2 brackets the two maquettes' relative sizes. In Scene B, locator 3 outlines the flat vertical color field around the centered larger maquette; locator 4 follows the ground line met by both maquettes.">Unmarked state. Scene A shows two repeated blue-gray studio maquettes: a larger maquette stands low at the left, while a smaller maquette stands higher with its feet where floor seams meet. Scene B shows the same size ratio: both maquettes meet one ground line, and a flat vertical color field surrounds the centered larger maquette.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Relative size has no automatic meaning. Receding lines and different ground positions can support a distance reading; shared ground and a central field can support visual emphasis. Claims about importance need contextual evidence.</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three artworks · three ways to weigh the evidence</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="brueghel" aria-pressed="true">1 · Brueghel</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="raemkai" aria-pressed="false">2 · Raemkai</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="kiyomitsu" aria-pressed="false">3 · Kiyomitsu</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="brueghel" data-desc-unmarked="Brueghel selected, unmarked state. Large foreground travelers and animals overlap along a broad road. The road curves and narrows toward smaller, paler figures and a distant city." data-desc-guided="Brueghel selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows repeated travelers diminishing along the road. Locator 2 follows the road narrowing among overlapping forms. Locator 3 surrounds the paler distant field and city.">
<p class="work-meta">National Gallery of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Jan Brueghel the Elder · Wooded Landscape with Travelers</h4><p class="object-meta">1610 · oil on panel · 37 × 58 cm · 2020.26.1</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the road from the nearest travelers toward the city. Besides size, what changes as the figures seem farther away?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A wooded landscape seen from above. Large travelers, animals, and carts overlap along a broad foreground road. The road curves and narrows toward smaller, paler figures and a distant city near the horizon.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c1/nga-2020.26.1-brueghel-wooded-landscape-travelers.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 4096 2587" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M510 2280 Q1110 2050 1660 1860 Q2070 1680 2350 1420 Q2460 1290 2530 1180"/><path class="guide-solid" d="M1180 2490 Q1770 2050 2320 1490 M3400 2490 Q2910 2050 2520 1490"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="2260" cy="1190" rx="380" ry="190"/><circle class="marker" cx="500" cy="2040" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="500" y="2040">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="3320" cy="2200" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="3320" y="2200">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2680" cy="1050" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="2680" y="1050">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Repeated figures</strong> — travelers and animals diminish along the curving road.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Road and overlap</strong> — nearer forms overlap as the broad road narrows inward.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Distant field</strong> — the smallest figures and city are paler and less distinct.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> figures repeat at diminishing sizes; <strong>2</strong> overlap and the narrowing road link those changes to recession; <strong>3</strong> distant forms lose contrast and detail. Relative size supports distance here because several other clues agree with it — this is converging recession, not a single size rule.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="raemkai" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Raemkai selected, unmarked state. An orienting whole-wall photograph shows stacked registers ending at part of a much larger figure on the right. A separate detail shows the tomb owner with two small sons beside his legs." data-desc-guided="Raemkai selected, guided state. A labeled detail shows the right side of the whole wall. Locator 1 marks the owner crossing several registers at the terminal edge. Locator 2 marks adult figures in smaller register scenes. Locator 3 follows their rightward orientation toward the owner.">
<p class="work-meta">Metropolitan Museum of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Tomb Chapel of Raemkai: East Wall</h4><p class="object-meta">Old Kingdom, Dynasty 5, ca. 2446–2389 BCE · limestone with paint · 08.201.1f</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Begin with the whole wall, then inspect the detail. Where does the large figure sit in relation to the registers — and what can size alone establish?</p>
<div class="raemkai-pair" role="group" aria-label="Two photographs of the same east wall: an orienting whole-wall view and a detail of the large tomb owner at the wall's right edge.">
<figure><p class="image-label">Whole wall · orientation view</p><div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="The east wall arranged in stacked horizontal registers. Rows of adult figures, animals, birds, and offerings extend toward the far-right edge, where part of a much larger standing figure crosses several registers."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c1/met-577371-raemkai-east-wall-whole.jpg" alt="" /></div></figure>
<figure><p class="image-label">Tomb owner · object-record detail</p><div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A detail showing the large standing tomb owner holding a staff, with two much smaller children beside his legs."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c1/met-577371-raemkai-tomb-owner-detail.jpg" alt="" /></div></figure>
</div>
<div class="guided-detail" data-guided-detail hidden><figure><p class="image-label">Guided crop · right side of the whole-wall photograph</p><div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A recorded crop from the whole-wall photograph. Adult figures occupy the upper and middle registers and face right. At the right edge, the tomb owner's much larger body crosses several registers."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c1/met-577371-raemkai-owner-adult-registers-crop.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1800 1500" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="guide-solid" x="1380" y="580" width="360" height="835"/><rect class="guide-line" x="30" y="150" width="1160" height="620"/><path class="guide-line" d="M300 270 H1280 M300 610 H1280"/><circle class="marker" cx="1320" cy="1010" r="48"/><text class="marker-text" x="1320" y="1010">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="90" cy="860" r="48"/><text class="marker-text" x="90" y="860">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1240" cy="420" r="48"/><text class="marker-text" x="1240" y="420">3</text></svg>
</div></figure></div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Exceptional scale</strong> — the owner crosses several registers at the wall's terminal edge.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Adult register figures</strong> — adults remain much smaller within the horizontal bands.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Orientation</strong> — those figures face toward the owner at the right, as the Met record confirms.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> one figure is exceptionally large and terminal; <strong>2</strong> adult figures occupy smaller register scenes; <strong>3</strong> their orientation directs attention toward the large figure. The Met identifies the large figure as the deceased tomb owner and the two figures beside his legs as his sons. The sons' smaller bodies are not independent proof of lower rank — the wall establishes prominence visually, and historical context establishes whose rank that prominence serves.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="kiyomitsu" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Kiyomitsu selected, unmarked state. Actors vary in size and overlap at different stage levels. A long rail and boards run diagonally, while horizontal bands, repeated trees, and costume patterns remain conspicuous across the sheet." data-desc-guided="Kiyomitsu selected, guided state. Locator 1 surrounds figure-size shifts and overlapping groups. Locator 2 follows the long diagonal rail. Locator 3 follows repeated horizontal stage bands.">
<p class="work-meta">Metropolitan Museum of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Torii Kiyomitsu · The Curfew at Dōjōji</h4><p class="object-meta">ca. 1763 · woodblock print; ink and color on paper · 31.1 × 43.2 cm · JP694</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the diagonal rail, then compare it with the horizontal bands and repeated patterns. Where do you sense depth, and where do you notice the print's surface?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A Japanese woodblock print of actors arranged on a striped stage. Figures vary in size and overlap at different stage levels. A rail and boards run diagonally, while bold horizontal bands, repeated trees, and costume patterns remain conspicuous across the flat sheet.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c1/met-37141-kiyomitsu-curfew-dojoji.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3882 2779" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="2190" cy="1750" rx="620" ry="880"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="1390" cy="910" rx="1000" ry="610"/><path class="guide-solid" d="M1510 720 L3760 1680"/><path class="guide-line" d="M120 1200 H1460 M120 1505 H1700 M120 1790 H1950"/><circle class="marker" cx="2780" cy="2100" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="2780" y="2100">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="3380" cy="1530" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="3380" y="1530">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="350" cy="1380" r="54"/><text class="marker-text" x="350" y="1380">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Scale and overlap</strong> — figure sizes shift as bodies overlap at different stage levels.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Diagonal</strong> — the long rail and boards can carry the eye backward.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Surface pattern</strong> — bands, trees, and costume patterns keep attention moving across the sheet.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> size shifts and overlap can support partial recession; <strong>2</strong> the long diagonal strengthens that cue; <strong>3</strong> repeated bands and patterns keep the surface active. The cues do not settle into a clean "distance" or "importance" answer — the Met describes a tension between two- and three-dimensionality, which is a catalogue interpretation, not evidence that this spatial system is inaccurate or historically behind another one.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Brueghel selected, unmarked state. Large foreground travelers and animals overlap along a broad road. The road curves and narrows toward smaller, paler figures and a distant city.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<p class="view-limit"><strong>Comparison note:</strong> These works offer different spatial systems to examine, not stages in artistic progress.</p>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Relative size does not explain itself. It may support distance, prominence, or both, but only when other visible relations — and sometimes historical context — make that reading persuasive.</p></div></div>
