<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c2" style="--acc:#4F6B4D;--acc-line:#4F6B4D;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#4F6B4D 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c2" data-screen-label="c2-aftermath-attention-action">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><circle cx="20" cy="9" r="4"/><path d="M20 13 L20 26 M8 33 L20 26 L32 20 M14 33 L20 26"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C2 · Aftermath, Attention, Action</span><h2 class="lens-title">How can a physically still body imply a moment in time?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A sculpted body does not move, but visible ground contact, direction, gesture, and nearby objects can support different inferences about time.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One maquette family · three relations to time</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Notice the feet, the body's direction, the arms, and where the same small sphere appears. Which relationships make each scene feel like a different moment?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="comparison cols-3">
<div><p class="scene-label">Scene A</p><div class="scene landscape" style="background:#d5cfc4 url('/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-neutral-room-v1.png') center/cover no-repeat" role="img" aria-label="A blue-gray studio maquette stands upright with level shoulders, parallel legs, and lowered arms. An ochre sphere meets the ground to its right, beside the same narrow support now shown tipped diagonally.">
<svg class="construction" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M90 623 H910" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.5)" stroke-width="4"/><ellipse cx="770" cy="623" rx="43" ry="11" fill="rgba(52,43,34,.2)"/><path d="M635 611 L728 523" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.58)" stroke-width="16"/><path d="M606 620 L664 620" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.58)" stroke-width="12"/><circle cx="770" cy="589" r="34" fill="#ce8141"/></svg>
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:30%;left:35%;bottom:.6%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c2/c2-maquette-aftermath-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="710" cy="566" rx="126" ry="82"/><circle class="marker" cx="842" cy="498" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="842" y="498">1</text></svg>
</div></div>
<div><p class="scene-label">Scene B</p><div class="scene landscape" style="background:#d5cfc4 url('/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-neutral-room-v1.png') center/cover no-repeat" role="img" aria-label="The same blue-gray studio maquette remains upright with arms lowered, but its head and upper torso turn toward an ochre sphere resting on a narrow stand at the right while its legs form an uneven stance.">
<svg class="construction" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M90 623 H910" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.5)" stroke-width="4"/><rect x="782" y="292" width="16" height="331" fill="rgba(91,77,60,.55)"/><ellipse cx="790" cy="623" rx="35" ry="9" fill="rgba(52,43,34,.18)"/><circle cx="790" cy="258" r="34" fill="#ce8141"/></svg>
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:30%;left:35%;bottom:.1%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c2/c2-maquette-attention-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M455 132 L555 225"/><circle class="guide-line" cx="790" cy="258" r="49"/><circle class="marker" cx="618" cy="170" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="618" y="170">2</text><path class="guide-line" d="M430 354 L392 616 M558 356 L585 616"/><circle class="marker" cx="398" cy="510" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="398" y="510">3</text></svg>
</div></div>
<div><p class="scene-label">Scene C</p><div class="scene landscape" style="background:#d5cfc4 url('/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c1/c1-neutral-room-v1.png') center/cover no-repeat" role="img" aria-label="The same blue-gray studio maquette stands with its feet meeting the ground far apart. One diagonal rises through a leg and torso while an opposing diagonal follows the raised arms; the directions cross, and the hands frame the same ochre sphere near one shoulder.">
<svg class="construction" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M90 623 H910" fill="none" stroke="rgba(91,77,60,.5)" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="445" cy="150" r="34" fill="#ce8141"/></svg>
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="figure" style="width:30%;left:35%;bottom:-.4%" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c2/c2-maquette-action-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-solid" d="M340 620 H655 M340 620 v-24 M655 620 v-24"/><circle class="marker" cx="495" cy="620" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="495" y="620">4</text><path class="guide-line" d="M430 610 L525 195"/><path class="guide-line" d="M420 125 L510 220"/><circle class="guide-line" cx="445" cy="150" r="49"/><circle class="marker" cx="566" cy="250" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="566" y="250">5</text></svg>
</div></div>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Grounded sphere and tipped support</strong> — the sphere meets the ground beside the narrow support shown on its side.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Local turn</strong> — head and upper torso angle to the same side as the sphere. <strong>3 · Uneven stance</strong> — the legs do not share one symmetrical axis.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>4 · Wide ground contact</strong> — the feet meet the ground far apart. <strong>5 · Crossing directions</strong> — a leg-and-torso diagonal crosses an opposing raised-arm diagonal while the hands frame the sphere.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In Scene A, <strong>1 the sphere meets the ground beside the tipped support</strong> while the upright body's arms remain lowered — that visible consequence relation may support an inference of aftermath, but it does not prove what happened. In Scene B, <strong>2 the head and upper torso angle to the same side as the sphere</strong> while <strong>3 the legs form an uneven stance</strong>; those relations may support alert attention, not a visible gaze or decision. In Scene C, <strong>4 the feet meet the ground far apart</strong> while <strong>5 a leg-and-torso diagonal crosses an opposing raised-arm diagonal</strong> as the hands frame the sphere — that may support a moment immediately before action, without showing a release or inventing a trajectory.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. In Scene A, locator 1 surrounds the sphere meeting the ground and the tipped support beside it. In Scene B, locator 2 marks the local head/torso axis and circles the sphere on the upright support; locator 3 follows the uneven leg axes. In Scene C, locator 4 brackets the wide-set feet; locator 5 marks the crossing diagonal and circles the sphere framed by the hands.">Unmarked state. Scene A shows a maquette standing upright with lowered arms while a sphere meets the ground beside a tipped support. Scene B shows the same maquette with head and torso angled toward a sphere on an upright support and legs in an uneven stance. Scene C shows the maquette with feet far apart, crossing diagonals through leg/torso and raised arms, and hands framing the sphere.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>A still body can imply a moment in time. A grounded object beside a displaced support may suggest aftermath; a body angled toward an object may suggest alert attention; wide ground contact and crossing directions may suggest a moment before action. These are possible readings, not visible facts.</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three artworks · three ways of holding time</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="nydia" aria-pressed="true">1 · Nydia</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="archers" aria-pressed="false">2 · Archers</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="pieta" aria-pressed="false">3 · Pietà</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="nydia" data-desc-unmarked="Nydia selected, unmarked state. The marble figure inclines forward, cups one hand beside her ear, grips a staff in the lowered hand, plants one foot more fully, and raises the heel of the other." data-desc-guided="Nydia selected, guided state. Locator 1 surrounds the hand cupped beside the ear and the inclined head. Locator 2 surrounds the lowered hand gripping the staff. Locator 3 follows the bodily incline and brackets the unequal foot contact.">
<p class="work-meta">Metropolitan Museum of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Randolph Rogers · Nydia, the Blind Flower Girl of Pompeii</h4><p class="object-meta">1853–54; carved 1859 · marble · 137.2 × 64.1 × 94 cm · 99.7.2</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Where do the hand, head, staff, and feet direct the body at the same time?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A museum photograph of Randolph Rogers's marble Nydia. The figure inclines forward, cups one hand beside her ear, grips a staff in the lowered hand, plants one foot, and raises the heel of the other foot.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-11951-nydia-blind-flower-girl-pompeii.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3008 3863" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="865" cy="1050" rx="285" ry="330"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="820" cy="1945" rx="250" ry="260"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1140 760 Q1510 1510 1870 2820"/><path class="guide-solid" d="M1255 3360 H2250 M1255 3360 v-58 M2250 3360 v-58"/><circle class="marker" cx="470" cy="860" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="470" y="860">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="430" cy="1945" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="430" y="1945">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2385" cy="3240" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="2385" y="3240">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Listening hand</strong> — one hand cups beside the ear as the head inclines.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Staff hand</strong> — the lowered hand grips the staff; contact between the staff and ground is not shown.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Incline and unequal feet</strong> — the torso leans while one foot is more fully planted.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> a hand cups beside the ear; <strong>2</strong> the lowered hand grips the staff; <strong>3</strong> the forward incline and unequal foot contact continue through the body. Together, these relations can suggest listening attention within continued movement. They do not show a sound source, a stop, or an inner decision.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="archers" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Archers selected, unmarked state. The central pair of relief figures face left with bow-holding arms extended, opposite hands drawing strings back, and arrow nocks remaining at the drawn strings." data-desc-guided="Archers selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows the extended bow-holding arms of the central pair. Locator 2 follows the visible carved strings back toward the drawing hands. Locator 3 surrounds the two points where arrow nocks remain at the drawn strings.">
<p class="work-meta">Metropolitan Museum of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Archers</h4><p class="object-meta">Old Kingdom, Dynasty 4, ca. 2551–2494 BCE · limestone with paint · 25.4 × 37.5 cm · 22.1.23</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> What remains held between the hands? What has not happened yet?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A museum photograph of an ancient Egyptian limestone relief fragment. The central pair of archers face left with bow-holding arms extended, opposite hands drawing back, and arrow nocks remaining at the drawn strings.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-543895-old-kingdom-archers.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2000 1600" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M590 890 Q825 850 1045 930 M1070 935 Q1355 930 1690 975"/><path class="guide-solid" d="M800 350 L1000 867 M1190 438 L1560 908"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="1000" cy="867" rx="82" ry="58"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="1528" cy="908" rx="86" ry="60"/><circle class="marker" cx="470" cy="850" r="36"/><text class="marker-text" x="470" y="850">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1020" cy="455" r="36"/><text class="marker-text" x="1020" y="455">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1720" cy="760" r="36"/><text class="marker-text" x="1720" y="760">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Extended arms</strong> — the bow-holding arms of the central pair reach forward.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Drawn strings</strong> — solid lines follow the carved diagonals back toward the drawing hands.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Held nocks</strong> — dashed rings mark where the arrow nocks remain at the drawn strings.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> the central pair extend their bow-holding arms; <strong>2</strong> the visible strings remain drawn back; <strong>3</strong> the arrow nocks remain at the drawing hands. The relief can therefore suggest imminent action: tension is prepared, but release, target, path, and impact are not shown.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="pieta" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Pietà selected, unmarked state. An upright seated figure supports a dependent body across her lap, including its head and upper torso, intact forearm, and lower limbs." data-desc-guided="Pietà selected, guided state. Locator 1 surrounds the supported head and upper torso. Locator 2 follows the supporting arms and lap. Locator 3 follows the intact dependent forearm and lower limbs, which do not bear the body.">
<p class="work-meta">Metropolitan Museum of Art · Public domain</p><h4>Hubert Gerhard · Pietà</h4><p class="object-meta">After 1595 · bronze · 29.5 × 16.5 × 19.1 cm · 2013.966</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Compare the supported and supporting bodies. What makes one body seem to depend on the other for support?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A museum photograph of Hubert Gerhard's bronze Pietà. An upright seated figure supports a dependent body across her lap, including his head and upper torso, intact forearm, and lower limbs.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-627606-gerhard-pieta.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3073 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M560 1720 Q585 1475 800 1435 Q1025 1445 1080 1640"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1010 1680 Q1310 1600 1620 1870"/><path class="guide-line" d="M720 2140 Q1260 2480 1900 2260"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1380 1700 Q1870 1850 2290 2040 M1590 2320 Q1980 2670 2190 3200"/><circle class="marker" cx="525" cy="1470" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="525" y="1470">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1190" cy="2590" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="1190" y="2590">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2390" cy="2590" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="2390" y="2590">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Supported head and torso</strong> — the reclining body rests across the upright figure.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Supporting arms and lap</strong> — hands, arms, and lap receive its weight.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Dependent limbs</strong> — the intact forearm and lower limbs extend without bearing the body.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1</strong> the head and upper torso recline across another body; <strong>2</strong> the upright figure's arms and lap provide support; <strong>3</strong> the intact forearm and lower limbs remain dependent rather than weight-bearing. These relations support an inference of aftermath. The title identifies Christ after death in Mary's lap; later physical damage to this object is not evidence for that represented event.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Nydia selected, unmarked state. The marble figure inclines forward, cups one hand beside her ear, grips a staff, plants one foot more fully, and raises the heel of the other.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<p class="view-limit"><strong>Comparison note:</strong> Relief and sculpture in the round offer different kinds of bodily evidence. These works are alternatives to compare, not stages in a chronology of progress.</p>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Still bodies can place us after an event, attending within movement, or just before release. The inference comes from visible support, gesture, foot contact, bodily direction, and what remains held — not from how twisted, lifelike, or historically late a work is.</p></div></div>
