</details>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c3" style="--acc:#7C4459;--acc-line:#94586E;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#94586E 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c3" data-screen-label="c3-what-light-means">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M6 34 L18 34 L18 22 Z"/><circle cx="26" cy="20" r="7"/><path d="M30 8 L36 30"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C3 · What Light Means</span><h2 class="lens-title">What can light do besides describe a scene?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A bright area does not automatically describe a lamp, sunlight, or an emotion. It can organize the surface, help a form appear to turn, or lead the eye through selected parts of a scene.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One recurring object set · three visible organizations of brightness</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Choose one bright relation in each panel. Does it surround objects, change across a form, or cross selected objects? What might that relation help you perceive — and what meaning can it not establish by itself?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="scene wide" role="img" aria-label="Three created panels repeat a blue-gray bowl, clay-colored sphere, folded paper arch, and tabletop in comparable, not identical, views. The left has a broad warm field behind the objects. The center has gradual light-to-dark changes across the objects. The right has a diagonal bright zone crossing selected objects while surrounding space stays dark.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c3/c3-light-field-form-path-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="guide-zone" x="34" y="72" width="432" height="500" rx="8"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="650" cy="680" rx="110" ry="75"/><ellipse class="guide-line" cx="808" cy="730" rx="56" ry="62"/><path class="guide-line" d="M780 690 C790 565 830 515 878 524 C930 535 955 620 962 704"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M1513 55 L1513 228 L1190 760 L1075 746 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="92" cy="126" r="28"/><text class="marker-text" x="92" y="126">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="955" cy="475" r="28"/><text class="marker-text" x="955" y="475">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1425" cy="285" r="28"/><text class="marker-text" x="1425" y="285">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key cols-1" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Field</strong> — a broad warm area sits behind the recurring objects on the left.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Modelling</strong> — gradual value changes are visible across the bowl, sphere, and folded arch in the center.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Path</strong> — a narrow diagonal bright zone crosses selected objects on the right while surrounding space stays dark.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relations:</strong> <strong>1</strong> a warm field surrounds the objects without showing a source; <strong>2</strong> light-to-dark transitions cross object surfaces; <strong>3</strong> a diagonal zone of brightness crosses selected objects amid darkness. <strong>Perceptual inferences:</strong> the field can unify the arrangement, the transitions can support a reading of turning volume, and the diagonal contrast can direct attention. <strong>Meaning boundary:</strong> none of those effects establishes sacred presence, a particular time of day, knowledge, feeling, or intent. The left-to-right order compares alternatives; it is not progress from flatness to realism.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. Locator 1 outlines the broad warm field behind the recurring objects on the left. Locator 2 groups the bowl, sphere, and folded paper arch whose surfaces show gradual value changes in the center. Locator 3 traces the diagonal bright zone through selected objects on the right.">Unmarked state. Three created panels repeat a bowl, sphere, folded arch, and tabletop in comparable views: a broad warm field at left, gradual value change in the center, and a diagonal bright zone crossing selected objects at right.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Before deciding what light means, name the visible relation: broad field, value transition, or directional contrast. Then ask what it may help you perceive — unity, turning volume, or an attentional path. Larger claims need subject, material, setting, and context.</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three visible relations, three historical works</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="utamaro" aria-pressed="true">1 · Utamaro</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="daumier" aria-pressed="false">2 · Daumier</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="fragonard" aria-pressed="false">3 · Fragonard</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="utamaro" data-desc-unmarked="Utamaro selected, unmarked state. Empty paper ground holds two black-haired figures, pale faces, and patterned robes in a broad field." data-desc-guided="Utamaro selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows open paper ground beside the larger head and robe. Locator 2 identifies open ground beside the lower figure's head.">
<p class="work-meta">Japan · Edo period · ca. 1790 · pictorial ground</p><h4>Two Women — Kitagawa Utamaro</h4><p class="object-meta">Woodblock print; ink and color on paper · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, JP2735</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the empty paper ground around the black hair, pale faces, and patterned robes. Does it behave like a source in a depicted place — or like part of the print's visible surface?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A Japanese woodblock print of two women. Empty paper ground surrounds black-haired figures, pale faces, and patterned robes.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-52002-utamaro-two-women.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1336 1941" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M88 120 L565 120 L565 230 M88 120 L88 1210"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M1120 540 L1235 540 L1235 1220"/><circle class="marker" cx="120" cy="180" r="38"/><text class="marker-text" x="120" y="180">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1190" cy="600" r="38"/><text class="marker-text" x="1190" y="600">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> open paper ground beside the larger head and robe.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> open ground beside the lower figure's head.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> empty paper ground holds the two figures, pale faces, and patterned robes in a broad field. <strong>Bounded inference:</strong> that contrast can keep the image visibly surface-like rather than making it read as one measured source in a depicted place. <strong>Meaning boundary:</strong> it does not, by itself, establish sunlight, a lamp, a real location, or either figure's identity or feeling.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="daumier" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Daumier selected, unmarked state. Darker and lighter wash values cross the two drinkers' faces against a broader dark field." data-desc-guided="Daumier selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows shifting wash values across the nearer drinker's face. Locator 2 marks changing values across the second drinker's face.">
<p class="work-meta">France · 1860–79 · value modelling</p><h4>Two Drinkers — Honoré Daumier</h4><p class="object-meta">Pen and ink with wash over charcoal on paper · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1975.1.601</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Trace a light-to-dark change across either forehead, nose, or cheek. What makes the two heads appear to turn rather than read as a single flat wash?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A wash drawing of two drinkers. Darker and lighter values model each head against a dark field.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-460035-daumier-two-drinkers.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3090 3942" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M785 1340 C870 1110 1270 1060 1490 1260 C1660 1420 1615 1740 1400 1880 C1130 2070 790 1910 740 1660 C710 1520 730 1420 785 1340 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M900 1490 C1090 1370 1300 1380 1470 1510 M1020 1700 C1180 1810 1320 1790 1450 1670"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M1650 1410 C1780 1160 2150 1120 2370 1320 C2560 1490 2500 1840 2260 1970 C2010 2110 1700 1940 1640 1700 C1615 1580 1610 1490 1650 1410 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="720" cy="1210" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="720" y="1210">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="2490" cy="1230" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="2490" y="1230">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> shifting wash values across the nearer drinker's face.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> changing values across the second drinker's face.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> darker and lighter wash values cross each drinker's forehead, nose, and cheek against the broader dark field. <strong>Bounded inference:</strong> those transitions can support a reading of two faces turning in space. <strong>Meaning boundary:</strong> they do not by themselves prove a particular physical source, the drinkers' character, mood, biography, or the artist's intention.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="fragonard" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Fragonard selected, unmarked state. Dark trees enclose a path while a small bright opening makes a fountain and nearby figures more available." data-desc-guided="Fragonard selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows dark tree enclosure at left. Locator 2 surrounds the small bright opening with fountain and figures. Locator 3 follows dark tree enclosure at right.">
<p class="work-meta">France · ca. 1775 · selective attention path</p><h4>A Shaded Avenue — Jean Honoré Fragonard</h4><p class="object-meta">Oil on wood · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 49.7.51</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the dark trees and low path toward the small bright opening. What makes that distant fountain and its nearby figures available before you know a narrative?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A dark, tree-lined avenue opens onto a small bright clearing with a distant fountain and small figures.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c2-c3/met-436324-fragonard-shaded-avenue.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3000 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M210 520 C620 250 1110 410 1310 920 C1450 1280 1310 1720 950 1990"/><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="1120" cy="3250" rx="430" ry="300"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M2650 1020 C2870 1490 2810 2200 2470 2670"/><circle class="marker" cx="250" cy="480" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="250" y="480">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1500" cy="3200" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="1500" y="3200">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2710" cy="960" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="2710" y="960">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> dark tree enclosure at left.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> small bright opening with fountain and figures.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3</strong> dark tree enclosure at right.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> dark trees enclose the path at both sides while a small bright opening makes the fountain and nearby figures more available. <strong>Bounded inference:</strong> the eye can connect enclosure and opening as a selective route into the scene. <strong>Meaning boundary:</strong> the route does not prove a physical beam, an exact time of day, a narrative event, or the painting's emotional meaning.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Utamaro selected, unmarked state. Empty paper ground holds two figures, pale faces, and patterned robes.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<p class="view-limit"><strong>Comparison boundary:</strong> this nonchronological order tests an evidence chain, not progress, naturalism, or expressive intensity. Each work contains other light and dark relations, and no single work represents a culture or period.</p>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>In real artworks, brightness can act as a ground, a way of turning form, or a route of attention. Begin with the visible relation, then state only the effect it can support. Subject, material, setting, and context are still needed before a larger claim.</p></div></div>
