<p>Jean Honoré Fragonard, <em>A Shaded Avenue</em>, ca. 1775, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 49.7.51, public domain. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436324">Object record</a>.</p>
</details>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c4" style="--acc:#8E5B6D;--acc-line:#B58193;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#B58193 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c4" data-screen-label="c4-when-surface-speaks">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="6" y="6" width="28" height="28"/><path d="M6 16 L20 6 M6 26 L30 6 M12 34 L34 12 M22 34 L34 22"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C4 · When Surface Speaks</span><h2 class="lens-title">When does surface describe a material, the artwork itself, or both?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A surface can describe glass, cloth, or stone inside an image. It can also be paint, paper, wood, or another material that makes an artwork a physical thing. Some artworks make both roles active at once.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One patterned-cloth motif · three surface roles</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the blue crisscross motif across the three panels. What makes it read first as a woven tablecloth, then as visible paint, and finally as both a fabric fragment and a tablecloth?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="scene wide" role="img" aria-label="Three created panels repeat an indigo textile with cream diamond-and-cross motifs. At left, a hyperrealistic still life shows the folded textile as a tablecloth beneath a cup. In the center, layered paint-like marks rebuild the textile pattern as an abstraction. At right, a collage-inspired frayed textile fragment sits beneath a cup, its edge modelling material presence.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c4/c4-surface-textile-roles-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1624 969" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M36 354 C145 300 365 315 512 410 L516 950 L25 950Z"/><rect class="guide-zone" x="556" y="22" width="514" height="926"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M1135 180 C1260 125 1480 165 1565 250 L1575 875 C1470 946 1225 944 1100 815Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="88" cy="337" r="29"/><text class="marker-text" x="88" y="337">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1026" cy="100" r="29"/><text class="marker-text" x="1026" y="100">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1543" cy="210" r="29"/><text class="marker-text" x="1543" y="210">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key cols-1" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Depicted material</strong> — woven fibers, folds, and the cup's weight make the blue motif read as a tablecloth inside a still life.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Painterly surface</strong> — the same motif is rebuilt from broad, layered-looking marks; paint-like handling becomes part of what you notice.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Both at once</strong> — a collage-inspired frayed fragment reads as a tablecloth beneath the cup while its separate edge models material presence.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Three roles</span><p><strong>1</strong> surface can describe a material in a pictured world. <strong>2</strong> painterly marks can make the surface carrying an image active in its own right. <strong>3</strong> a collage-inspired fragment can model how incorporated fabric may remain materially present while helping to make a still life. These are alternatives to compare, not stages from less to more advanced art.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. Locator 1 surrounds the woven cloth beneath the cup in the hyperrealistic still life. Locator 2 frames the abstract center panel, where layered-looking marks rebuild the cloth motif. Locator 3 follows the collage-inspired frayed textile beneath the cup, modelling how actual material could remain present.">Unmarked state. Three created panels repeat an indigo textile motif: as a depicted tablecloth, as painterly marks, and as a collage-inspired fragment that can read as both at once.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>When you notice texture, sheen, or pattern, ask what role it is playing. Is it describing something within the image? Is it making the artwork's own material presence noticeable? Or does it do both at once?</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three surface roles, three historical works</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="melendez" aria-pressed="true">1 · Meléndez</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="monet" aria-pressed="false">2 · Monet</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="tapestry" aria-pressed="false">3 · Tapestry</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="melendez" data-desc-unmarked="Meléndez selected, unmarked state. White cloth, grapes, melon, bread, and cookware are differentiated through changes in paint." data-desc-guided="Meléndez selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies the folded white cloth. Locator 2 identifies the clustered grapes. Locator 3 identifies the netted rind and cut flesh of the melon.">
<p class="work-meta">Spain · ca. 1772 · pictured materials</p><h4>The Afternoon Meal (La Merienda) — Luis Meléndez</h4><p class="object-meta">Oil on canvas · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1982.60.39</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Move from the white cloth to the basket, grapes, melon, bread, and reflective cookware. What changes in the paint help each passage read as a different kind of thing?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A still life with white cloth, grapes, melon, bread, and cookware, each rendered with distinct paint handling.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/met-437053-melendez-afternoon-meal.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1200 827" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="952" cy="454" rx="142" ry="113"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="672" cy="336" rx="162" ry="132"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="572" cy="641" rx="96" ry="126"/><circle class="marker" cx="1084" cy="390" r="27"/><text class="marker-text" x="1084" y="390">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="757" cy="221" r="27"/><text class="marker-text" x="757" y="221">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="475" cy="571" r="27"/><text class="marker-text" x="475" y="571">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> folded white cloth.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> clustered grapes.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3</strong> netted rind and cut flesh of the melon.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> the cloth gathers broad pale folds, the grapes collect small glints, and the melon changes from netted rind to soft exposed flesh. <strong>What this supports:</strong> changes in edge, highlight, color, and pattern can make paint describe materially different things in a pictured world. <strong>Keep looking:</strong> the same painting also makes a composition; material description alone does not settle its larger subject or meaning.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="monet" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Monet selected, unmarked state. Short, varied marks gather into two stacks and redirect through field and sky." data-desc-guided="Monet selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies the large stack, built from short varied marks. Locator 2 identifies the smaller stack. Locator 3 follows field passages where marks redirect across the ground.">
<p class="work-meta">France · 1890–91 · paint made active</p><h4>Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer) — Claude Monet</h4><p class="object-meta">Oil on canvas · Art Institute of Chicago, 1985.1103</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Let your attention move across the large stack, the smaller stack, the field, and the sky. Where do repeated, varied marks remain noticeable while also building the view?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="Two wheat stacks in a field, painted with short, varied, visible brushmarks.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/aic-64818-monet-stacks-wheat.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1600 945" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="1201" cy="511" rx="199" ry="250"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="410" cy="535" rx="125" ry="120"/><path class="guide-line" d="M126 735 C375 690 612 779 873 744 C1111 713 1320 787 1510 722"/><circle class="marker" cx="1372" cy="321" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="1372" y="321">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="300" cy="423" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="300" y="423">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="129" cy="654" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="129" y="654">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> large stack built from short, varied marks.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> smaller stack built differently at a distance.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3</strong> field passages where marks redirect across the ground.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> short, layered-looking marks gather into the stacks, then spread and redirect through the field and sky. <strong>What this supports:</strong> paint does not simply disappear behind the wheat; its handling stays active while it helps make the landscape. <strong>Keep looking:</strong> noticeable marks do not by themselves tell us an artist's feeling, a single moment of weather, or the full meaning of the view.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="tapestry" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Tapestry-Woven Fragment selected, unmarked state. Colored wool makes a horse, decorative motifs, and a patterned band while irregular edges keep the fragment present as a woven textile." data-desc-guided="Tapestry-Woven Fragment selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies the woven horse motif. Locator 2 identifies the green patterned band. Locator 3 follows an irregular frayed woven edge.">
<p class="work-meta">Found Egypt · possibly Iraq, Iran, or Caucasus · 8th century · material and image</p><h4>Tapestry-Woven Fragment</h4><p class="object-meta">Wool; slit-tapestry weave with eccentric wefts · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2001.420</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the horse, the small decorative motifs, and the green patterned band. How does the same woven material make both the fragment's physical presence and its image?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A woven tapestry fragment depicting a horse and a green patterned band, with irregular frayed edges.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/met-454035-tapestry-woven-fragment.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 551 1200" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="177" cy="602" rx="88" ry="73"/><rect class="guide-zone" x="80" y="795" width="373" height="115" rx="12"/><path class="guide-line" d="M132 138 C102 282 125 400 95 504 M454 412 C488 506 464 620 484 716"/><circle class="marker" cx="287" cy="523" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="287" y="523">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="451" cy="770" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="451" y="770">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="88" cy="111" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="88" y="111">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> woven horse motif.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> green patterned band.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3</strong> irregular, frayed woven edge.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> colored wool structures the horse and patterned band, while irregular edges and the same interlaced material keep the fragment present as a made textile. <strong>What this supports:</strong> here the material is not merely shown by an image; it is also what makes the image. <strong>Keep looking:</strong> that coexistence is one surface relation to compare, not a claim that this work is more direct, advanced, or truthful than the paintings.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Meléndez selected, unmarked state. White cloth, grapes, melon, bread, and cookware are differentiated through changes in paint.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<p class="view-limit"><strong>Comparison note:</strong> this is an analytical order, not a history of progress. Each work contains more than one surface relation, and no single work stands for an entire culture or period.</p>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>When texture, sheen, pattern, or a material edge catches your attention, ask what role it is playing. Is it describing something within an image? Is it making the artwork's own medium noticeable? Or are those two roles active together?</p></div></div>
