<p>Claude Monet, <em>Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)</em>, 1890–91, Art Institute of Chicago, 1985.1103, CC0. <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/64818/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer">Object record</a>.</p>
<p><em>Tapestry-Woven Fragment</em>, 8th century, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2001.420, public domain. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/454035">Object record</a>.</p>
</details>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c5" style="--acc:#4E6B50;--acc-line:#5F8060;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#5F8060 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c5" data-screen-label="c5-carved-modelled-cast-constructed">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M8 34 L8 18 L18 12 L18 34 Z"/><circle cx="27" cy="24" r="8"/><line x1="27" y1="8" x2="27" y2="16" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C5 · Carved, Modelled, Cast, Constructed</span><h2 class="lens-title">What can evidence tell us about how a sculpture was made — and what stays unresolved?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A sculpture can hold clues about material being removed, shaped, transferred through a mold, or joined. Those clues help us ask how it came together; they do not tell its whole making story alone.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One invented form, four ways of coming together</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the same tall upright, diagonal element, and low rounded mass across the worktable. Where does the form seem to come from one mass, soft addition, a transfer through a mold, or separately joined parts?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="scene wide" role="img" aria-label="Four invented sculptures repeat a tall upright, diagonal element, and low rounded mass: a stone-like carved form, a clay-like modelled form, a bronze-like form with two mold halves, and a steel-like constructed form with joined parts.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c5/c5-making-sequences-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1672 941" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M164 710 L164 454 L281 426 L381 575 L344 709 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M503 702 C540 598 615 564 692 606 C728 632 739 700 715 748 C654 785 544 771 503 702 Z"/><rect class="guide-zone" x="808" y="300" width="505" height="490" rx="12"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1401 350 L1401 612 M1450 448 L1545 625 M1354 754 L1618 754"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="1442" cy="443" rx="34" ry="31"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="1547" cy="636" rx="39" ry="34"/><ellipse class="guide-zone" cx="1398" cy="622" rx="40" ry="30"/><circle class="marker" cx="152" cy="403" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="152" y="403">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="602" cy="525" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="602" y="525">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1137" cy="262" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="1137" y="262">3</text><circle class="marker" cx="1584" cy="534" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="1584" y="534">4</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key cols-1" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> a faceted recess within a continuing stone-like mass.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> soft clay-like volumes pressed together.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3</strong> a bronze-like form beside two mold halves.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>4</strong> distinct steel-like rods and plates meeting at welded junctions.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Four general relations</span><p><strong>1</strong> a carved form can keep a relation to material removed and material remaining. <strong>2</strong> modelling can build a form through added, pressed, and reshaped material. <strong>3</strong> casting can transfer a model through a mold into a new material. <strong>4</strong> construction can make separately formed parts and their joins active. These visual models identify questions to take to an artwork and its sources; they do not reconstruct any particular maker's full sequence.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. Locator 1 marks a faceted recess within a continuing stone-like mass. Locator 2 marks soft clay-like volumes pressed together. Locator 3 identifies a bronze-like form beside two mold halves. Locator 4 marks steel-like rods and plates meeting at welded junctions.">Unmarked state. Four invented sculptures repeat a tall upright, diagonal element, and low rounded mass, varying as stone-like carved, clay-like modelled, bronze-like cast, and steel-like constructed forms.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>When a plane, added volume, mold relation, seam, or join catches your eye, name what you can see first. Then ask which source could connect that feature to a particular making sequence — and which parts of the story remain unresolved.</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three works, three evidence chains</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="rodin" aria-pressed="true">1 · Rodin</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="remington" aria-pressed="false">2 · Remington</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="inca" aria-pressed="false">3 · Inca figurine</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="rodin" data-desc-unmarked="Rodin selected, unmarked state. More fully worked figures meet rougher marble that remains part of the same group." data-desc-guided="Rodin selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies rougher marble at the right and lower edge. Locator 2 identifies more fully worked figures.">
<p class="work-meta">France · modelled 1889, carved ca. 1908–09 · marble</p><h4>Pygmalion and Galatea — Auguste Rodin</h4><p class="object-meta">Marble · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 10.31</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Move from the comparatively smooth figures to the rougher marble at the right and lower edge. Where does the work keep its relation to the larger stone mass?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A marble sculpture group where more fully worked figures meet rougher, unfinished marble at the edges.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/met-191292-rodin-pygmalion-galatea.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 4000 3276" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M2580 1350 C2920 1240 3440 1590 3540 2130 L3440 3030 L2400 3040 L2170 2530 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M1570 690 C1960 390 2550 580 2680 1050 L2580 2470 L1800 2580 L1430 1970 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="3330" cy="1300" r="46"/><text class="marker-text" x="3330" y="1300">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1780" cy="600" r="46"/><text class="marker-text" x="1780" y="600">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> rougher marble along the right and lower edge.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> more fully worked figures within the group.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> more fully worked figures meet rougher, more irregular marble that remains part of the same group. <strong>What the record adds:</strong> the Met dates the work as modelled in 1889 and carved around 1908–09. <strong>What remains open:</strong> the visible contrast does not reveal every step, tool, or person involved in moving from model to marble.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="remington" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Remington selected, unmarked state. Rider, horse, rope, and base read as one continuously shaped bronze group." data-desc-guided="Remington selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows the interconnected rider-and-horse form. Locator 2 identifies the broad bronze base.">
<p class="work-meta">United States · 1895, cast 1918 · bronze</p><h4>The Broncho Buster — Frederic Remington</h4><p class="object-meta">Bronze; cast by Roman Bronze Works · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1986.81.2</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the rider's bent body, the horse's lifted head, and the broad base. What makes these separate motions read as one continuous bronze form?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A bronze sculpture of a rider on a rearing horse, cast as one continuous form on a broad base.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/met-11860-remington-broncho-buster.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2953 3884" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M730 720 C1120 470 1780 500 2140 850 C2400 1120 2380 1720 2100 2050 C1750 2330 1070 2250 700 1840 C490 1530 510 1020 730 720 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M760 3240 C1180 3140 2110 3170 2470 3360 L2380 3630 C1890 3710 1020 3690 700 3510 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="520" cy="720" r="46"/><text class="marker-text" x="520" y="720">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="2360" cy="3000" r="46"/><text class="marker-text" x="2360" y="3000">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> interconnected rider-and-horse form.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> broad bronze base that gathers the action.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> the rider, horse, rope, and base read as a single, continuously shaped bronze group. <strong>What the record adds:</strong> this 1918 bronze was cast by Roman Bronze Works; the Met also documents that editions of the subject used both sand casting and later lost-wax casting. <strong>What remains open:</strong> the visible bronze surface alone does not identify a mold, distinguish those casting methods, or account for the whole edition history.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="inca" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Inca female figurine selected, unmarked state. A vertical hair line and small changes in the gold surface organize the compact figure." data-desc-guided="Inca female figurine selected, guided state. Locator 1 follows the vertical line and engraved texture in the hair. Locator 2 identifies the compact shaped gold of the head, torso, and arms.">
<p class="work-meta">Inca · 1400–1533 · formed and joined gold sheet</p><h4>Female figurine</h4><p class="object-meta">Gold · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1979.206.1058</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the vertical line down the center of the hair and the figure's compact head, torso, arms, and legs. Which lines and shifts in the gold seem important enough to investigate?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="A small gold Inca figurine with a compact head, torso, and arms, and a vertical line down the center of the hair.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/met-313251-inca-female-figurine.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2786 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M1400 525 C1390 830 1420 1100 1405 1410"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M920 560 C1110 390 1690 390 1880 620 L1800 2320 L1000 2320 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="1135" cy="480" r="42"/><text class="marker-text" x="1135" y="480">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1860" cy="1570" r="42"/><text class="marker-text" x="1860" y="1570">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> vertical line and engraved texture in the hair.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> compact head, torso, and arms made from shaped gold.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> a vertical hair line and small changes in the gold's raised and incised surface give the tiny figure a precise, organized form. <strong>What the technical study adds:</strong> X-radiography and other 2017 examination identified five separately formed sheet components and solder in most joined areas. <strong>What remains open:</strong> whether pressure welding was also used remains unresolved; the visible line alone does not prove the complete construction sequence.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Rodin selected, unmarked state. More fully worked figures meet rougher marble that remains part of the same group.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>A rough edge, continuous surface, hair line, or base can give you something specific to investigate. Name the visible feature first. Then ask what a museum record, technical study, or other source can add — and what questions it still cannot answer.</p></div></div>
<details class="credit"><summary>Image credits and sources</summary>
<p>Auguste Rodin, <em>Pygmalion and Galatea</em>, modelled 1889, carved ca. 1908–09, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 10.31, public domain. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/191292">Object record</a>.</p>
<p>Frederic Remington, <em>The Broncho Buster</em>, 1895, cast 1918, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1986.81.2, public domain. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/11860">Object record</a>.</p>
<p><em>Female figurine</em>, Inca, 1400–1533, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1979.206.1058, public domain. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/313251">Object record and technical note</a>.</p>
</details>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="c6" style="--acc:#566F5D;--acc-line:#7B9883;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#7B9883 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-c6" data-screen-label="c6-object-viewer-site">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M12 34 L12 8 L28 8 L28 34 M12 21 L4 21 M28 21 L36 21"/><circle cx="20" cy="21" r="3"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">C6 · Object, Viewer, Site</span><h2 class="lens-title">How can a documented placement organize encounters among object, viewer, and site?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Placement is part of what we see. The same object can be framed by a threshold, raised above a pictured viewer, or seen from an oblique position in an open room.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Controlled study</span><em>One invented form, three placements</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Find the same tapered form, rounded base, and diagonal plane in all three settings. Where does the doorway align a view? Where do height and distance change the relation? Where does the room make a side position available?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" data-simple>
<div class="scene wide" role="img" aria-label="The same invented sculpture appears three times: framed by a doorway, raised on a tall plinth above a pictured viewer, and seen obliquely from the side in an open gallery.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/created-illustrations-v2/cross-period-c6/c6-placement-encounters-v1.png" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1672 941" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M62 35 L471 35 L471 849 L63 849 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M610 180 L996 180 L996 866 L610 866 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M1178 343 C1305 315 1510 332 1618 470 L1618 782 L1180 782 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="90" cy="92" r="31"/><text class="marker-text" x="90" y="92">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="701" cy="215" r="31"/><text class="marker-text" x="701" y="215">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1204" cy="365" r="31"/><text class="marker-text" x="1204" y="365">3</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key cols-1" hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1 · Threshold</strong> — the doorway centers the object along an approach.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2 · Height and distance</strong> — a tall plinth separates the pictured viewer from the raised form and changes their eye-level relation.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>3 · Open room</strong> — an oblique position and space at the side make a lateral view available.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Three placement relations</span><p><strong>1</strong> a threshold can frame an object and align a particular view with an entrance. <strong>2</strong> a plinth can establish height and distance between an object and a viewer. <strong>3</strong> an open room can make an oblique or lateral view available. The represented sculpture has not changed; the object, viewer, and site are arranged differently. Each setting can make one relation more immediate without making it the only possible encounter.</p></div>
<p class="sr-only" data-unmarked aria-live="polite" data-guided="Guided state. Locator 1 frames the doorway and its centered approach to the sculpture. Locator 2 identifies the raised sculpture, tall plinth, and pictured viewer below. Locator 3 identifies the sculpture, low plinth, and the pictured adult's oblique position in the open gallery.">Unmarked state. The same invented sculpture appears three times: through a doorway, raised on a tall plinth above a pictured viewer, and at an oblique side position in an open gallery.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="concept" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>When you look at sculpture, begin with one relation: a doorway framing it, a base raising it, or room around it. Then ask how that placement organizes the encounter you are having with the object.</p></div></div>

<div class="arc-divider">In Real Art</div>
<section class="realart" data-selector>
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Three works, three site states</h3></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<nav class="work-select" aria-label="Choose an artwork and site state"><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="dendur" aria-pressed="true">1 · Temple of Dendur</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="liberty" aria-pressed="false">2 · Statue of Liberty</button><button class="wt-btn" type="button" data-work="flamingo" aria-pressed="false">3 · Flamingo</button></nav>
<article class="work" data-panel="dendur" data-desc-unmarked="Temple of Dendur selected, unmarked state. A tall sandstone gateway frames a smaller opening deeper in the temple, surrounded by a raised gallery floor, pool, and glass wall." data-desc-guided="Temple of Dendur selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies the tall gateway and smaller entrance beyond it. Locator 2 identifies the raised gallery platform and pool in front of the temple.">
<p class="work-meta">Present museum-gallery state · Gallery 131</p><h4>The Temple of Dendur</h4><p class="object-meta">Roman Period · completed by 10 B.C. · Aeolian sandstone · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 68.154</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Move from the tall gateway at the right to the smaller entrance beyond it, then out to the pool and glass wall. Which visible parts frame an approach through the temple, and which organize its present gallery setting?</p>
<div class="scene natural portrait" role="img" aria-label="The Temple of Dendur in its Metropolitan Museum gallery, with a tall gateway, pool, and glass wall.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/c6-object-viewer-site/met-547802-temple-dendur-gallery-131.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2995 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M650 885 L2835 885 L2835 2890 L650 2890 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M80 2170 C830 2020 1700 2130 2620 2680 L2570 3920 L80 3920 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="1590" cy="850" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="1590" y="850">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="205" cy="2140" r="58"/><text class="marker-text" x="205" y="2140">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> tall gateway and smaller entrance beyond it.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> raised gallery platform and pool in front of the temple.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> the tall gateway frames a smaller opening deeper in the temple, while a broad floor, pool, and glass wall create a new surrounding setting. <strong>What the record adds:</strong> the Met identifies this as the Temple of Dendur, completed by 10 B.C. and now shown in Gallery 131. <strong>Site state:</strong> this is a present museum-gallery encounter with the temple, not its original Nubian setting.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="liberty" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Statue of Liberty selected, 2017 photographed site state, unmarked. The raised figure and tall pedestal sit above Liberty Island, with water and boats extending around the monument." data-desc-guided="Statue of Liberty selected, guided state. Locator 1 contains the figure and tall pedestal. Locator 2 identifies the island edge, water, and boats below and beyond it.">
<p class="work-meta">Selected photograph's setting · Liberty Island · 2017</p><h4>Liberty Enlightening the World — Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi</h4><p class="object-meta">1886 · copper sculpture and pedestal · Liberty Island, New York Harbor</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the figure down through the tall pedestal to the island edge, then outward across the water to the boats. How do height, water, and the base organize distance in this photographed setting?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="The Statue of Liberty on its tall pedestal, seen from across the water with boats visible near the island.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/c6-object-viewer-site/loc-highsm-45295-statue-liberty-island-2017.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1024 683" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M292 72 C405 22 560 48 590 212 L557 474 L325 481 L262 312 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M80 395 C330 355 690 390 1010 350 L1010 670 L80 670 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="271" cy="90" r="36"/><text class="marker-text" x="271" y="90">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="142" cy="402" r="36"/><text class="marker-text" x="142" y="402">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> figure and tall pedestal.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> island edge, water, and boats below and beyond the monument.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> the raised figure and tall pedestal sit above the island edge, while water and boats establish a broad space around the monument. <strong>What the record adds:</strong> the Library of Congress identifies this selected photograph as Carol M. Highsmith's 2017 view of the Statue of Liberty on Liberty Island. <strong>Site state:</strong> this page studies that documented 2017 island-and-harbor view.</p></div>
</article>
<article class="work" data-panel="flamingo" hidden data-desc-unmarked="Flamingo selected, 2017 photographed site state, unmarked. The open red steel form stands on broad pavement among tall building fronts in Federal Center Plaza." data-desc-guided="Flamingo selected, guided state. Locator 1 identifies the open red steel form against the building grid. Locator 2 identifies the broad pavement and bollard line.">
<p class="work-meta">Selected photograph's setting · Federal Center Plaza · 2017</p><h4>Flamingo — Alexander Calder</h4><p class="object-meta">1974 · steel · Chicago Federal Center Plaza · U.S. General Services Administration</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the red steel's open legs and arch, then the pavement, bollards, and building fronts around it. How does the sculpture hold a place in this broad city plaza?</p>
<div class="scene natural" role="img" aria-label="A large open red steel sculpture standing on a broad paved plaza among tall building fronts.">
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/cross-period/historical/c4-c6/c6-object-viewer-site/loc-highsm-63037-calder-flamingo-2017.jpg" alt="" />
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1024 401" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M604 46 C732 14 960 30 1002 111 L1002 386 L570 386 L570 232 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M18 276 L998 276 L998 390 L18 390 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="594" cy="90" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="594" y="90">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="95" cy="258" r="34"/><text class="marker-text" x="95" y="258">2</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="guide-key" data-guide-key hidden><p class="key-item"><strong>1</strong> open red steel form against the building grid.</p><p class="key-item"><strong>2</strong> broad pavement and bollard line that give the plaza a shared ground.</p></div>
<div class="caption" data-caption hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>Visible relation:</strong> the open red steel form meets a broad paved plaza and a field of tall building fronts rather than a separate pedestal or room. <strong>What the record adds:</strong> GSA records <em>Flamingo</em> as a 1974 steel work installed at Chicago Federal Center; the Library of Congress dates this photograph to 2017. <strong>Site state:</strong> this is a selected 2017 plaza view, with the sculpture and surrounding federal architecture visible together.</p></div>
</article>
<p class="sr-only" data-desc aria-live="polite">Temple of Dendur selected, unmarked state. A tall sandstone gateway frames a smaller opening, surrounded by a raised gallery floor, pool, and glass wall.</p>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" data-kind="connection" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>A gallery setting, an island pedestal, and a city plaza make different relations available. Start with the frame, base, ground, water, or architecture you can see. Then name the particular site state the record identifies before making a claim about an encounter.</p></div></div>
<details class="credit"><summary>Image credits and sources</summary>
