<p>Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, <em>Liberty Enlightening the World</em>, 1886. Photograph: Carol M. Highsmith, 24 August 2017, Library of Congress, LC-DIG-highsm-45295; no known restrictions on publication. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017882044/">Photograph record</a>.</p>
<p>Alexander Calder, <em>Flamingo</em>, 1974, steel, Chicago Federal Center Plaza. Photograph: Carol M. Highsmith, 27 July 2017, Library of Congress, LC-DIG-highsm-63037; no known restrictions on publication. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/item/2020721899/">Photograph record</a>; <a href="https://art.gsa.gov/artworks/23671/flamingo">GSA artwork record</a>.<span class="rights-note">The Highsmith photograph documents the plaza and its surroundings. Reproduction of Calder's underlying 1974 sculpture for external publication has a separate, unresolved rights review; this page presents it only within that documented site photograph, consistent with the module's release status.</span></p>
</details>
</section>
</section>
</div>

</div>
</div>

<div class="bank-divider">
<div class="eyebrow-strip"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Recurring Subjects</div>
<h2>Recurring Subjects</h2>
<p>Some looking questions begin with a relation—scale, light, surface, making, or site. Others begin with a recurring subject: a place, a person, or an event. Choose any study; none is a survey or a required sequence.</p>
</div>

<div class="chooser-group wide" data-group="recurring-subjects">
